</section>

<section id="ideal-device" class="band band--tint">
  <h2 class="section-title">A wish list for an electronic conspicuity device</h2>
  <p>Here are the parts we&rsquo;d ask a manufacturer to try in a device that shares your position and listens for nearby aircraft. It&rsquo;s a wish list. We have no plans to build it, and it isn&rsquo;t for sale. skyBlip supports Go and Bravo on their current boards.</p>

  <div class="table-scroll">
    <table class="prose">
      <thead>
        <tr><th scope="col">Role</th><th scope="col">Part</th><th scope="col">Why we&rsquo;d try it</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">Processor</th><td>Nordic nRF54LM20A</td><td>It runs at 128&nbsp;MHz with 512&nbsp;KB of RAM and up to 66 GPIO, enough for two or three radios. Its native USB lets a UF2 bootloader run on the chip itself, as on today&rsquo;s nRF52840 boards, with no bridge chip: copy a file to the device to reinstall it. We haven&rsquo;t found a UF2 bootloader for the nRF54 yet.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">ADS-L radio</th><td>Semtech LR2021 at 868&nbsp;MHz</td><td>Send and receive ADS-L. The datasheet lists 27&nbsp;mA when transmitting at +14&nbsp;dBm.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">ADS-B receiver</th><td>Second LR2021 at 1090&nbsp;MHz</td><td>Bravo already uses this chip to receive ADS-B at 1090&nbsp;MHz. That is outside the specified 150 to 960&nbsp;MHz range. We&rsquo;d ask for a receive circuit with measured performance at 1090&nbsp;MHz.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Optional third radio</th><td>Third LR2021 in the 868&nbsp;MHz band</td><td>Try listening on the other ADS-L channel or for messages from the ground, including while the first radio transmits. We haven&rsquo;t measured how much this would help.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Satellite positioning</th><td>u-blox NEO-F10N or MAX-M10S</td><td>The NEO-F10N tracks two bands, L1 and L5, at 21&nbsp;mA. The L1-only MAX-M10S draws about 12&nbsp;mA while tracking.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Air pressure</th><td>Goertek SPA06-003, as on the X1, or Bosch BMP581</td><td>Both match or beat the MS5611 that open-source varios use. The SPA06-003 reads 0.3&nbsp;Pa RMS at 64x, the BMP581 0.08&nbsp;Pa at its highest setting. Both are accurate to &plusmn;0.3&nbsp;hPa.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Motion</th><td>ST LSM6DSV16X</td><td>Motion can wake the processor. The accelerometer alone draws 17 to 20&nbsp;&micro;A in low-power mode.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Screen</th><td>Memory LCD or e-paper, up to 4.7&nbsp;inches</td><td>The Sharp LS027B7DH01 LCD is 2.7&nbsp;inches at 400&nbsp;&times;&nbsp;240 pixels. It updates faster and works down to -20&nbsp;&deg;C. Good Display&rsquo;s GDEY042T81 e-paper offers better contrast and takes 0.3&nbsp;s to refresh part of the screen.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Alerts</th><td>TI DRV2605L, LRA vibration motor and buzzer</td><td>Give pilots an alert they can feel and hear.</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">Storage and power</th><td>8&nbsp;MB QSPI NOR flash, a 2000 to 2500&nbsp;mAh high-voltage LiPo cell like the X1&rsquo;s, and a TI BQ27220 or similar battery gauge</td><td>Store data in flash and track battery charge as it flows in and out.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>From datasheets, we estimate a draw of about 41&nbsp;mA with two radios and 48&nbsp;mA with three, at the datasheets&rsquo; 3.0 to 3.3&nbsp;V. The sum counts the NEO-F10N tracking at 21&nbsp;mA. Each 868&nbsp;MHz receiver counts 5.7&nbsp;mA in FSK on its DC-DC converter. The 1090&nbsp;MHz receiver counts 7.9&nbsp;mA, the LR2021&rsquo;s figure for 2&nbsp;Mbps MSK, the closest to ADS-B. Each radio&rsquo;s oscillator adds 1.5&nbsp;mA. The processor counts about 2&nbsp;mA and a Bluetooth link about 1&nbsp;mA. The sensors and a memory LCD stay under 0.5&nbsp;mA, and transmitting under 0.1&nbsp;mA. The MAX-M10S would save about 9&nbsp;mA. We haven&rsquo;t measured any of these figures on a board.</p>

  <p>Connect the satellite receiver&rsquo;s pulse-per-second signal and the sensors&rsquo; interrupt lines to the processor. Share one small antenna, like the Go&rsquo;s, between 868 and 1090&nbsp;MHz through a diplexer, with an option for an external antenna. Give a third radio its own small antenna. Run each radio on its built-in DC-DC converter. Put a 1/4&Prime; threaded insert in the centre of the back, as on the Go+, so the device clicks onto a quick release. Publish the EU declaration of conformity and the Radio Equipment Directive test reports.</p>

  <p>If you build boards at Seeed Studio or LilyGO, we&rsquo;d like to hear what you could build and test. Write to <%= mail_to Rails.configuration.x.contact_email %>.</p>
</section>
